[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Synchronization in Core Java: Locks, Mutexes, and Semaphores"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In concurrent programming, synchronization is crucial for managing access to shared resources by multiple threads. Without proper synchronization, race conditions, data corruption, and other undesirable behaviors can occur. Java provides several mechanisms for achieving synchronization, including locks, mutexes (which are essentially locks), and semaphores. This tutorial will explore these concepts in detail with code examples."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Concurrency Issues"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into synchronization mechanisms, it's essential to understand the problems they solve. Consider a simple counter incremented by multiple threads:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class Counter {
    private int count = 0;

    public void increment() {
        count++;
    }

    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

class IncrementingThread extends Thread {
    private Counter counter;

    public IncrementingThread(Counter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
            counter.increment();
        }
    }
}

public class ConcurrencyProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Counter counter = new Counter();
        IncrementingThread thread1 = new IncrementingThread(counter);
        IncrementingThread thread2 = new IncrementingThread(counter);

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        thread1.join();
        thread2.join();

        System.out.println("Final count: " + counter.getCount());
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You might expect the final count to be 20000. However, due to race conditions (multiple threads accessing and modifying `count` simultaneously), the result is often less than 20000. This happens because the `count++` operation is not atomic; it involves reading the current value, incrementing it, and writing the new value. Multiple threads can interleave these steps, leading to lost updates."},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"Locks and Mutexes"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A lock (or mutex) is a synchronization primitive that allows only one thread to access a shared resource at a time. In Java, the `synchronized` keyword and the `java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock` interface provide mechanisms for using locks."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the `synchronized` Keyword"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `synchronized` keyword can be used to protect critical sections of code. It can be applied to methods or blocks of code. When a thread enters a `synchronized` block or method, it acquires a lock associated with the object (or the class object for static methods). Other threads attempting to enter the same `synchronized` block or method will be blocked until the first thread releases the lock."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class SynchronizedCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
    }

    public synchronized int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

class SynchronizedIncrementingThread extends Thread {
    private SynchronizedCounter counter;

    public SynchronizedIncrementingThread(SynchronizedCounter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
            counter.increment();
        }
    }
}

public class SynchronizedExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        SynchronizedCounter counter = new SynchronizedCounter();
        SynchronizedIncrementingThread thread1 = new SynchronizedIncrementingThread(counter);
        SynchronizedIncrementingThread thread2 = new SynchronizedIncrementingThread(counter);

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        thread1.join();
        thread2.join();

        System.out.println("Final count: " + counter.getCount());
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `increment()` and `getCount()` methods are synchronized, ensuring that only one thread can access and modify the `count` variable at a time. This prevents race conditions and guarantees the correct final count."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Using `java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock`"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock` interface provides more flexible locking mechanisms than the `synchronized` keyword.  `ReentrantLock` is a common implementation of the `Lock` interface."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

class LockCounter {
    private int count = 0;
    private Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

    public void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            count++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public int getCount() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            return count;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}

class LockIncrementingThread extends Thread {
    private LockCounter counter;

    public LockIncrementingThread(LockCounter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
            counter.increment();
        }
    }
}

public class LockExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        LockCounter counter = new LockCounter();
        LockIncrementingThread thread1 = new LockIncrementingThread(counter);
        LockIncrementingThread thread2 = new LockIncrementingThread(counter);

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        thread1.join();
        thread2.join();

        System.out.println("Final count: " + counter.getCount());
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, a `ReentrantLock` is used to protect the `increment()` and `getCount()` methods. It's crucial to release the lock in a `finally` block to ensure that the lock is always released, even if an exception occurs.  `ReentrantLock` provides more control than `synchronized`, allowing for features like fair locking and timed lock attempts."},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"Semaphores"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"A semaphore is a synchronization primitive that controls access to a limited number of resources. It maintains a count representing the number of available resources. Threads can acquire a permit (decrement the count) to access a resource and release a permit (increment the count) when they are done. Semaphores are useful for limiting the number of threads that can concurrently access a resource."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

class SemaphoreResource {
    private Semaphore semaphore;

    public SemaphoreResource(int permits) {
        this.semaphore = new Semaphore(permits);
    }

    public void accessResource(int threadId) throws InterruptedException {
        semaphore.acquire();
        try {
            System.out.println("Thread " + threadId + " accessing resource...");
            Thread.sleep((long) (Math.random() * 1000)); // Simulate resource usage
            System.out.println("Thread " + threadId + " releasing resource...");
        } finally {
            semaphore.release();
        }
    }
}

class SemaphoreThread extends Thread {
    private SemaphoreResource resource;
    private int threadId;

    public SemaphoreThread(SemaphoreResource resource, int threadId) {
        this.resource = resource;
        this.threadId = threadId;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            resource.accessResource(threadId);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}

public class SemaphoreExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SemaphoreResource resource = new SemaphoreResource(2); // Allow 2 concurrent accesses

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            SemaphoreThread thread = new SemaphoreThread(resource, i);
            thread.start();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `SemaphoreResource` is initialized with 2 permits, allowing only two threads to access the resource concurrently. The `acquire()` method blocks until a permit is available, and the `release()` method releases a permit.  The output will show that only two threads are accessing the resource at any given time."},{"order":21,"type":"heading","text":"Choosing the Right Synchronization Mechanism"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`synchronized`: Simple and easy to use for basic synchronization needs. Suitable for protecting small critical sections of code. Implicitly reentrant."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Lock` (e.g., `ReentrantLock`): Provides more flexibility and control than `synchronized`.  Allows for fair locking, timed lock attempts, and interruptible waiting. Requires explicit locking and unlocking."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Semaphore`: Useful for controlling access to a limited number of resources.  Allows a specified number of threads to access a resource concurrently."},{"order":25,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Synchronization is essential for writing correct and efficient concurrent programs in Java. Understanding the different synchronization mechanisms, such as locks, mutexes, and semaphores, is crucial for managing access to shared resources and preventing race conditions. Choose the appropriate mechanism based on the specific requirements of your application."}]
